Sprint 1 — Building the Foundation

Before writing a single line of new code, I want us to answer one engineering question:

What is the first thing our application should be able to do?

Many beginners answer:

"Show photos."

That sounds reasonable...

…but it's actually skipping several layers.

Think about a real library.

Library
│
├── Shelves
│     ├── Books
│     ├── Books
│     └── Books

Nobody throws books into a giant pile.

The shelves come first.

Only then do the books go onto a shelf.

Our gallery is identical.

Gallery
│
├── Albums
│      ├── Photos
│      ├── Photos
│      └── Photos

So before we can display a single photo...

...we need albums.

That is why Sprint 1 is not "Photos."

It is Albums.

What are we building?

At the end of Sprint 1, Version 1 should be able to do this:

Browser

Wedding Showcase
Holiday Party
Latin Showcase
Kids Program
Summer Camp

Clicking an album should eventually open its photos.

Notice something important.

There are no images yet.

We're only proving that our application can move album data from the database, through the backend, to the frontend.

That single flow teaches almost everything we'll need later.

Why start here?

Imagine you're testing a brand-new water system in a house.

You don't connect every sink, shower, washing machine, and sprinkler at once.

You first make sure water can travel from:

Water Tank
      ↓
Pipe
      ↓
Kitchen Faucet

Once one faucet works...

Every other faucet uses the same plumbing.

Our application is exactly the same.

