Max, this is a perfect example of why I want to teach you engineering thinking instead of just giving you answers.

We didn't immediately assume:

"Express is broken."

Instead we investigated.

What happened?
You had:

Old Node process
running in memory.

When you changed:

function handleHomePageRequest(request, response) {
    console.log(...);
}
the running Node process had no idea you edited the file.

It continued executing the old code.

Only after you did:

Ctrl + C
and then

node index.js
did Node read the updated index.js from disk.

This is an important concept
There are two copies of your program.

On disk
index.js
This is just a text file.

In memory
When you execute

node index.js
Node loads that file into memory.

Now it is a running program.

Changing the file does not magically change the program already running in memory.

Real-life analogy
Imagine you print a recipe.

recipe.docx
Then you start cooking.

Halfway through cooking you edit the Word document.

Does the food on the stove suddenly change?

😂

Of course not.

The cook is following the recipe he already printed.

To use the new recipe, he must start over.

That's exactly what Node does.

